 ## Overview
-Fast Dual Contouring is a surface extraction method that generates a manifold mesh from a voxel grid by identifying edge intersections and computing vertex positions via Quadratic Error Function (QEF) minimization [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from standard Dual Contouring by optimizing the grid traversal and QEF assembly for real-time performance, often implemented as a C++ plugin for engines like Unity [S1, unofficial]. Unlike Marching Cubes, which produces fixed topology per cell, this method creates a single vertex per feature (edge intersection) shared by multiple cells, allowing for sharper feature preservation and potential mesh simplification [S1, unofficial].
+Fast Dual Contouring is a surface extraction method that generates a manifold mesh from a voxel grid by identifying edge intersections and computing vertex positions via Quadratic Error Function (QEF) minimization [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from standard Dual Contouring by optimizing the grid traversal and QEF assembly for real-time performance, typically implemented as a C++ plugin for game engines like Unity [S1, unofficial]. Unlike Marching Cubes, which produces fixed topology per cell, this method creates a single vertex per feature (edge intersection) shared by multiple cells, allowing for sharper feature preservation and optional mesh simplification [S1, unofficial].
 
